#---
# purpose: "a lambda head pins a value it closed over"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # lambda_pins_capture
#
# A pin resolves against the bindings that existed before the pattern began.
# A lambda head has such bindings: whatever the lambda closed over. Those
# captures arrive as the generated function's leading inputs, so a pin in the
# head is compared against the input that delivers the capture.
#
# Three shapes share that one rule:
#
#   * a single-clause lambda pinning a local;
#   * a lambda returned from a function, pinning that function's parameter;
#   * a multi-clause lambda, whose clause selection happens in the body rather
#     than the head, so its pin resolves by name in the enclosing scope.
def main() do
  x = 1
  same = fn ^x -> :same end
  dbg(same.(1))

  dbg(pick(2).(2))

  choose = fn ^x -> :same
              _ -> :other end
  dbg(choose.(1))
  dbg(choose.(9))
end

defp pick(x), do: fn ^x -> :same end
